    my $n = 0;
    sub TIESCALAR { bless \(my $thing = pop), shift }
    sub FETCH { $n++; ${$_[0]} }
    tie $tied, main => $untied = [];
    $n = 0;
    eval { bless $tied, 'Q' };
    print "fetches: $n ref untied: ", ref $untied, "\n";

perl 5.40.3: `fetches: 1 ref untied: Q`
PCL (base 06a4b8ba and the s532d tree alike): `fetches: 2 ...` (the s532d tree gets `Q` right, base said `ARRAY`).

#408's diagnosis ("PCL blesses without invoking FETCH") was wrong: PCL does FETCH -- twice.  The class missing from $untied was #4061 (a first bless of an ARRAY referent lived on the box only).  What is left is the read count: p-bless's operand is read twice (probably once for the ref test and once for the referent).  Same family as #1813 (DECIDED: "AN OPERATOR READS ITS OPERAND ONCE") -- read the operand into a local once at the top of p-bless.  Probe: scratch/s532d/pr2/sw4.pl in the s532d worktree.


