MECHANISM (the task's guess "p-bless reads twice" was not it): `bless $tied, 'Q'` alone is ONE FETCH in PCL too.  The second FETCH was the EVAL frame's leave copy -- `eval { bless $tied, 'Q' }` returns the tied box and p-eval-block's `%p-leavesub` unboxed it although the eval was a whole void statement (perl copies nothing out of a frame left in void context; `eval { $tied };` FETCHes 0 in perl, 1 in PCL).
FIXED: `p-void-ctx` expands a SOLE `p-eval-block` body form into `p-eval-block-discarded`, which omits the leave copy; both macros expand through ONE helper `%p-eval-block-expansion` (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp).  Only the sole form qualifies: a consumed eval deeper in a void statement (`my $r = \eval { $x }`) keeps the copy -- %p-leavesub's docstring is the measurement.  No emission change (macroexpansion only).  ir-spec §6.3.
NOT FIXED: the same copy at a SUB frame (`sub f { bless $tied, 'Q' } f();` 2 vs 1) -- the callee cannot know, and the arguments of a void call share its binding; FILED #4804 with the design constraint.
